+<a id="native-message-decoration"></a>
+## Native message decoration
+
+Durable user and Assistant `file` blocks retain native file cards. The optional Session-scoped `conversation.chat.user.file-media` and `conversation.chat.message.file-media` positions receive `{ file: FileAttachmentRef }` after each card, allowing an audio plugin to append playback controls. Unknown Assistant content retains its JSON presentation.
+
+`ctx.uiChatChrome.contractVersion === 1` advertises identity-only message decoration. `registerAppearance({ id, priority?, select })` elects the first non-null pure selector in ascending rank (default 100), preserving registration order for ties; duplicate ids throw. Register through the contributing Context's effect and release its idempotent disposer on unload. A selector exception is reported and delegates without hiding native content. Configuration changes replace the registration, not the renderer. The `appearances` source is apply-world input for framework-bound hooks, not a component subscription API.
+
+The [`ChatMessageOwner`](src/client/contract/message-chrome.ts) names the current Session-scoped occurrence with `contractVersion`, stable `instanceKey`, `kind`, `part`, available node/message/call identities and Turn/Step coordinates. It includes the original producer-owned `source` when available, pending/echo flags, original reasoning `blockIndex`, and the identity-v1 `gutter` placeholder; placement geometry remains native-owned. Historical provider/model/effort data comes from the contributing plugin's Host projection through the standard `useProjection`; this package does not scan history or infer it from the current model setting.
+
+The official `conversation.chat.message.chrome` Factory declares single/session `conversation.chat.message.header` and `conversation.chat.message.avatar`. Both receive only `{ owner }` plus framework props, never the native body or a renderer continuation. The Factory reads appearance through its framework-bound `useChatChrome` hook. `uiChatChrome.avatarPlacementVersion === 1` accepts `avatarPlacement: 'header' | 'auto'` in an appearance; omission means header. Auto uses the current view's outer margin only when the native scroll container has at least 40px of unclipped space on the selected physical side. The existing viewport size observer publishes the native style-query facts independently for each live Chat or read-only preview. Narrow views and browsers without style-query support keep the header layout. Reasoning and atomic tool rows retain their inline avatars. The legacy `owner.gutter` remains `{ available: false, width: 0 }`: it contains no geometry and does not authorize contribution CSS to position content. Native chrome alone moves its own 28px avatar, without reserving a body column, changing body width, or intercepting width-handle gestures.
+
+Appearance selects `native`, `bubble`, or `transparent`, optional start/end header alignment, contribution-owned classes, and CSS custom properties. Bubble paint uses `--dsh-message-background`, `--dsh-message-border-color`, and `--dsh-message-radius` without adding padding, clipping, a scroll container, or a stacking context. Existing native wrappers retain their body outlet, keys, refs, anchors, visibility policy, and controls while the fixed chrome sibling changes independently. Node seats, complete reasoning disclosures, local echoes, and Host-pending inputs participate. Ungrouped Assistant response runs have fixed surfaces keyed by their first original block index, so mixed reasoning and replies retain independent frames as streaming grows. Turn controls and containing tool/reasoning seats are always transparent; atomic tools have their own non-nesting surfaces in [ui-tool](../ui-tool/README.md#rendering-contract).
+
+Official builds use the `ui-chat` settings namespace. A separately deployed Host entry may set the public build constant `DSH_CLIENT_CHAT_SETTINGS_NAMESPACE` to its entry id before building both faces; omission retains the official namespace. The deployer must explicitly copy the existing five preference values into the new entry and preserve the original settings. The override is artifact content, not a runtime setting or a profile mutation performed by this package.
+
