 ### Reading a settled question back
 
-Each answered batch appears once in a right-aligned user-style bubble after its answer is recorded, for native and PTC blocking questions and late replies. The bubble shows every question above its selected choices and custom answer, uses lighter question text, and stays visible when process groups or the containing Turn are folded. A blocking-question answer bubble does not prevent completed work steps from collapsing. Late replies admitted after the opening step are interleaved Turn inputs and keep that Turn open. Each bubble separates the process runs before and after its recorded answer. Unanswered questions remain in the question card and tool row.
+Each answered batch appears once in a right-aligned user-style surface after its answer is recorded, for native and PTC blocking questions and late replies. The default aggregates the batch into one native box; an individual presentation paints one native box per question-and-answer pair while retaining the same recorded batch. The bubble shows every question above its selected choices and custom answer, uses lighter question text, and stays visible when process groups or the containing Turn are folded. A blocking-question answer bubble does not prevent completed work steps from collapsing. Late replies admitted after the opening step are interleaved Turn inputs and keep that Turn open. Each bubble separates the process runs before and after its recorded answer. Unanswered questions remain in the question card and tool row.
 
 A question that already settled opens as a read-only card: the same pager over the recorded answers, marked `Answered`. Every option and text field is disabled, Skip and the submit action are gone, a question the user skipped says so in place of its empty answer, and a free-text field appears only where the recorded answer used one. Closing it removes the card, and the tool call row builds another from the same record.
 
+### Question presentation preferences
+
+`ctx.uiQuestionPresentation.contractVersion === 1` advertises a global view preference. Independent plugins call `registerPresentation({ id, priority?, mode })` through their Context's effect; `mode` is `aggregate` or `individual`. Lower priorities select first, the default priority is 100, ties preserve insertion order, duplicate ids throw, and the returned disposer is idempotent. No contribution receives questions, answer data, renderers, or settlement callbacks. The provider publishes `{ mode }` through its stable apply-world `presentation` source only when the selected mode changes; removing all contributions restores `aggregate`.
+
+The historical reply and Tool transcript keep their native content and controls mounted while the selected level paints boxes: the whole batch in aggregate mode, or each stable question id in individual mode, never both levels. A skipped item retains its localized skipped marker. The active composer already shows one question at a time and keeps its pager, draft storage, countdown, and single whole-batch answer channel in either mode. Plan review and permission decisions are unaffected; skipped answers remain blank answers, not authorization.
+
 ### The plan-review card
 
 A `plan-review` intent — set by `dsh-plan-mode` on the `exit_plan_mode` review — renders a compact approval card: a `Plan review` strip with a `View full plan` link, the plan title and a two-line plain-text summary, and `Request changes` / `Approve` actions. The strip uses the shared warning dot and changes it to the ongoing loader while either action settles. The complete plan opens in the sidebar through the link or its permanent Chat card. Approve answers with the asker's approval label; `Request changes` rejects the wait as `ASK_CANCELLED`, returning the composer for the user's feedback without submitting an approval. The card has no separate refusal button.
